<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palk Strait</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palk Strait is a shallow strait between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> peninsula of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> with Palk Bay. To its south the chain of shoals called Adam's Bridge or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> Setu runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannar">Mannar</a>. Named after Robert Palk, an 18th-century governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, it has long been a route for fishing, pearling and migration between the two lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palk-strait.jpg" alt="Palk Strait" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adams_bridge_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The strait stretches for about 137 km and is between 64 and 137 km wide. Its waters are shallow: around the shoals of Ram Setu they are typically only 1 to 3 metres deep, the central part is typically around 20 metres deep, and the greatest depth is 35 metres. To the north, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, the waves are mostly swells, while in Palk Bay to the south they are mostly smaller wind waves. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> the shoals are part of a bridge built by the vanara army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> to rescue his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> from the Asura king Ravana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Last Glacial Period, when sea levels fell to as much as 120 metres below their present values, the whole of the shallow strait was dry land. It was submerged again as the sea rose during the Holocene, by around 7,000 years ago.</p><p>The construction of a shipping channel through the strait was first proposed to the British government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1860. Pamban Island was linked to the Indian mainland by the Pamban Bridge, opened for traffic on 24 February 1914. Trains ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> to Dhanushkodi at the southern tip of the island, where a ferry crossed to Talaimannar on Mannar Island and another train carried passengers on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>. The railway line to Dhanushkodi was destroyed in the 1964 Dhanushkodi cyclone together with a passenger train, killing around 200 people. A ferry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> and Talaimannar continued into the 1970s and was discontinued by the Indian government in 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The shallows, small islands and reefs make the strait difficult for large ships, though fishing boats and small craft navigate it. The Sethusamudram Shipping Canal Project, commissioned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> in 2005 to dredge a channel, met opposition on religious grounds. A road bridge beside the Pamban Bridge was opened in 1988, and a new railway bridge was completed in 2024 after rail traffic on the old bridge was suspended permanently in February 2023 over concerns about its stability. A combined undersea tunnel and bridge linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, the Palk Strait Bridge, was first discussed in 2002 and has been examined several times since.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palk Strait” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sethupathis led a warrior society of Maravar clans and controlled the pilgrim route and pearl fisheries of the <b>Palk Strait</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ramnad-kingdom">Ramnad Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
